  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHeight rises aloft and increasing warm southwest surface flow\nstarting this evening (as a weak shortwave crosses north of \nMichigan) favors dry weather and rising temperature by Tuesday\nafternoon. Shorter period of record sites like New Philadelphia\nmay see highs that tie/break the daily record (20-30%\nprobability) while most others just experience highs that are\n10-15 degrees above the daily averages.\n\nSubtle embedded shortwaves along the northern ridge periphery\nwill push a cold front southward into Wednesday before stalling\napproximately near the I-70 corridor. This will favor a trend\ntoward more seasonable temperature into Thursday with rain\nshowers mainly Wednesday afternoon/evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nEnsemble models are favoring the development of a notable upper\nlevel trough that will dive southeast through the Great Lakes \nregion starting early Friday morning and into Saturday, with\ncluster analysis favoring a modestly steep but progressive\ntrough/surface low system. The model mean pattern would lend to\nrain with potentially advisory-level wind gusts (and/or gusty\nwind-driven showers) Friday, waning precipitation with breezy\nbut low-impact wind gusts Saturday, and a low probability\n(~10-15% chance) for lake-enhanced rain/snow showers Saturday\nmorning in northwest PA.\n\nCurrent guidance favors the west central PA to higher terrain\nlocations for wind gusts that reach Wind Advisory criteria (46\nmph or greater) with non-zero High Wind Warning gusts in terrain\ntops of eastern Tucker County. However, variance in frontal\ntiming and depth of post-frontal mixing are the primary\nindicators to monitor in whether wind risk become more/less\nlikely in the days ahead.\n\nPrevious discussion of the first snowflakes of the year (and\neven accumulation) have taken a hit in probabilities due to some\ncoalesce of modeled synoptic pattern of a more progressive\nsystem that both limits the degree of cold advection and\nduration of ideal lake-effect wind direction. Snow birds will\nwant to see ensemble trends toward a deeper, slower trough \n(mainly suggested by a handful of Canadian Ensemble Model \nmembers) in order to return higher probabilities of seeing the\nfirst flakes of the year.",
